This repository contains Zabbix templates and setup guides for agentless monitoring VRA / VCO


Both implementations use HTTP Agent items with JSONPath preprocessing to query native REST APIs directly.

Template 1: VMware Aria Automation (vRA)
Overview
This template monitors system health, database state, and cluster readiness for VMware Aria Automation 8.x environments. It automates the OAuth2 token lifecycle by using a long-lived Refresh Token to fetch short-lived Bearer Access Tokens.
Prerequisites
•	Zabbix Version: 6.4 or 7.0+
•	vRA Permissions: Organization Member or Cloud Admin read access
•	Network connectivity from Zabbix server/proxy to the vRA FQDN over HTTPS (443)
Step-by-Step Installation
1. Generate the vRA Refresh Token
1.	Log in to the VMware Aria / vRA Web Interface.
2.	Click your username in the top-right corner and select User Profile.
3.	Under API Tokens, click Generate API Token.
4.	Set a name (e.g., Zabbix Monitoring) and copy the generated token string.
2. Import the Template
1.	Save the vRA template YAML definition as vra_template.yaml.
2.	Navigate to Data collection > Templates in Zabbix.
3.	Click Import, select vra_template.yaml, and complete the import process.
3. Create the Host & Configure Macros
1.	Go to Data collection > Hosts > Create host.
2.	Enter your Host Name (e.g., vRA-Production).
3.	Link the template: VMware Aria Automation by HTTP.
4.	Select the Macros tab and set the following Host Macros:
Macro	Sample Value	Description
{$VRA.HOST}	vra.domain.local	FQDN or IP of your vRA appliance
{$VRA.REFRESH.TOKEN}	eyJhbGciOi...	The API token generated in Step 1
5.	Click Add.
Self-Signed Certificates: If your vRA environment uses self-signed SSL certificates, open the template's master HTTP items (vRA: Get Access Token and vRA: Raw Deployment Health) and set SSL verify peer to No.
Template 2: Standalone vRealize Orchestrator (vCO)
Overview
This template provides single-server monitoring for standalone vRealize Orchestrator instances. It tracks overall service readiness, active execution threads, and failed workflow counts using direct HTTP Basic Authentication.
Prerequisites
•	Zabbix Version: 6.4 or 7.0+
•	vCO Service Account: Dedicated user account with API access to the vCO Control Center/REST API
•	Network access to the vCO server on TCP 8281 (default API port)
Step-by-Step Installation
1. Set Up Credentials
1.	Ensure a local or domain service account (e.g., zabbix_mon) exists with read access to the vCO REST API.
2.	Verify that the vCO REST API endpoint is accessible at https://<vCO-IP>:8281/vco/api/health-status.
2. Import the Template
1.	Save the standalone vCO template YAML definition as vco_standalone_template.yaml.
2.	In Zabbix, go to Data collection > Templates.
3.	Click Import, select vco_standalone_template.yaml, and confirm.
3. Create the Host & Configure Macros
1.	Go to Data collection > Hosts > Create host.
2.	Enter your Host Name (e.g., vCO-Standalone-01).
3.	Link the template: VMware vRealize Orchestrator Standalone by HTTP.
4.	Go to the Macros tab and set the following Host Macros:
Macro	Sample Value	Description
{$VCO.HOST}	vco.domain.local	FQDN or IP of the vCO host
{$VCO.PORT}	8281	vCO API service port
{$VCO.USER}	zabbix_mon	Service account username
{$VCO.PASS}	SecretPassword	Service account password
5.	Click Add.
Verification & Troubleshooting
1.	Go to Monitoring > Latest data.
2.	Filter by the newly created hosts.
3.	Verify the following expected initial execution results:
o	vRA Host: The vra.get.access_token item populates a bearer string without error, and vra.health.status returns UP.
o	vCO Host: The vco.health.status item populates with status STARTUP_COMPLETED.
Common Issues
•	401 Unauthorized (vRA): Check that the {$VRA.REFRESH.TOKEN} hasn't expired and has sufficient organization roles.
•	401 Unauthorized (vCO): Verify the basic authentication credentials in {$VCO.USER} and {$VCO.PASS}.
•	Connection Refused / Timeout: Check firewall routes between Zabbix Server/Proxy and port 443 (vRA) or port 8281 (vCO)

